'''French Baliscans''' ({{Langx|bl|Franco-baliscanos}}; {{Langx|fr|Franco-baliscains}}) or '''Franco-Baliscans''' are citizens or nationals of [[Balisca]] who identify themselves as having full or partial French heritage, ethnicity, or ancestral ties. As of the 2025 census, there are approximately 132,000 French nationals residing in Balisca, and a further 6.4 million Baliscans that claim either full or partial French ancestry.

From the 16th to 18th century, French settlement in what is now Balisca was largely restricted to the northern coast of the island of [[Pindorama]] after being [[W:France Antarctique|driven out of Brazil]] by the Portuguese. Of these settlements, [[Sainte-Croix]] (present-day [[Maracaju]]) was the most successful. The first wave of French immigration occurred in the 1770s following France's territorial loses in the [[W:Seven Years' War|Seven Years' War]], with thousands of French people dispersing across [[Atlántida]]. Many others also settled in Cerdanya (specifically [[Concepción]], [[Merced]], [[Rosario]] and [[Muntanya]]). In later decades, several settlements were founded by Frenchmen in southern Cerdanya, such as [[Maine-au-Provence]] (named after the [[W:Maine (province)|traditional French region]]) and [[Villa Francesca|Villa Francesc]].

